[{:seon.task/name "writes-commit-before-any-wait"
  :seon.task/plan "An agent's run-ahead writes commit when it is idle (`seon.agent/transact!`), and before a merge, a clone or the run's end. A Seon agent's whole run runs inside one eval (`seon.turn/work!`), so it is never idle: a turn's writes stay uncommitted through the next model call, seconds to minutes, and commit only at the next turn's first write (measured: 19 commits over 335 evals on task-t5-edit-forms). A crash or kill in that window loses the record of what the agent just did, against the durability law (a write followed by an effect outside the database waits for its flush; a model call is such an effect). Correct: commit before any blocking wait: `seon.agent/fx!` (every effect), `seon.agent/run-tests-forked`, `seon.agent/await-op!`; then every turn is durable before the agent waits on the outside world. An answered write that crosses a process (the reply to root) is committed and flushed first: bin/check durable-read failed about 1 run in 3 (the clone :a4 answers its transact!, root SIGKILLs its process group, and after recovery :dur-clone holds no note; `seon.check/durable-write`). This task's proof includes durable-read 10 runs in a row."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/a-turn-is-committed-before-its-model-call}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-process-says-what-it-is"
  :seon.task/plan "In Activity Monitor and ps, every process a root forks (the fork server, its standby, each agent, clone and test child) shows the root's own command line, ./seon root PORT, so the owner can't tell a root from an agent or which checkout it belongs to (14 processes across five checkouts, all named alike). Correct: each process names itself by role and checkout when it starts, e.g. \"seon fork-server (next-wiki)\", \"seon agent a3 (next-wiki)\": on macOS by rewriting its argv area in place (what setproctitle does), so ps shows it; the same name is a fact on its entity. Part of the process principle (AGENTS §4). Proof: after spawning an agent, ps -o command= for each pid names its role."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-death-names-its-cause"
  :seon.task/plan "Reproduction: two runs ended `:closed` with reason \"agent a9 died\" and nothing more, while root's log held the causes: an uncaught `{:type :fatal-commit-error}` from Datahike in `seon.repl/record!`, and a ClassCastException (nil cannot be cast to IFn) in `seon.agent/run-tests-forked`, plausibly after the agent redefined a platform function its own loop calls (a real REPL may; the merge judges). The next run and root learn nothing from a bare death. Correct: the agent process's entrant boundary (`seon.agent`'s loop, `seon.fork-server/main` for a child) writes the uncaught exception as the run's end, the one error map (`:seon.error/type`, `:seon.error/operation`, message, cause chain) on its branch, before the process exits; root's judge carries it to the run's reason and evidence. Then find the fatal commit's cause from that evidence."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/an-agent-that-throws-ends-with-its-cause}} {:seon.task/name "an-agent-holds-mains-tests"
  :seon.task/plan "Done. Agent contexts showed every proof `:no-assertions` (263 of 265; notes/archive/AUDIT-2026-10-22-agents.md, broken thing 1) because `seon.program/held?` treated every form imported from the source tree as compiled into the binary, so `seon.program/load-changes!` (the fork server's :tx) never loaded test/'s namespaces. Now `held?` is \"this process holds the form's namespace\". A test namespace loads without effects: seon.merge-test's top-level def that opened the test store at load (in the single-threaded fork server the connect never returned) is a function. An agent's run selects what the done rule (`seon.reconcile/done-explain`) reads: `seon.agent/test-plan`, the task's accumulated proofs and `seon.program/reaching-tests` over `seon.program/changed-vars` (what the branch changed since the main it forked from), never main's whole suite."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/an-agent-holds-mains-tests-and-runs-them seon.agent-test/an-agents-test-run-is-its-proofs-and-what-its-change-reaches seon.merge-test/declared-data-merges-by-key}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-wide-reach-stays-cheap"
  :seon.task/plan "An agent's test run is its task's proofs plus `seon.program/reaching-tests` over what its branch changed (`seon.agent/test-plan`). A one-defn change reaches 1 test (9.9 ms the whole forked run), but a change to `seon.config/value` reaches 263 tests (359 ms to walk) and to `seon.print/text` 252, root-booting tests included, all run in the agent's fork. Correct: measure what such a run costs end to end and make it proportional to what changed, reusing results by reach digest (task seon.program/tests-run-by-reach)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-wide-reach-reuses-unchanged-results}}
 {:seon.task/name "memory-is-read-when-asked"
  :seon.task/plan "Done. `seon.agent/mem-mb` reads `lib/real-mb` (one task_info call) when asked, on the events that report the agent's state (its hello, each commit, a `:tests` reply, `:stats`), never per evaluation and never kept for a time: no cache keyed by time (AGENTS §5), no clock. It left docs/clocks.md's owed list."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/memory-is-read-when-asked}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-agents-base-is-its-merge-base"
  :seon.task/plan "Done. `seon.agent/merge-main!` sets the agent's `:base` to `seon.db/branch-base` (the merge base of its branch and main) after its merge commit, so test selection (`seon.agent/test-plan`, `seon.program/changed-vars`) reads against the main it merged, not the one it forked from. `seon.program/changed-vars` compares forms by :form/id (a merge applies main's forms by content under the branch's own entity ids, so ids alone counted every merged form as the branch's). The writer's :spawn names main's commit and t on :fork, and `seon.agent/init!` reports a branch or image elsewhere as :seon.agent/fork-misaligned with its hello (recorded as the agent's problem, `seon.agent-proc`). The fork server's replica of main stays (notes/archive/store-native-2026-10-22.md#q2): replaying a 20-datom commit into it costs 0.68-1.22 ms, while reading the whole of main (114k index datoms) from the store costs 316-398 ms per cold process, and the replica is what keeps the program compiled (`seon.program/load-changes!`)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/after-merge-main-the-agent-judges-against-the-new-base
                      seon.procs-test/a-fork-names-the-commit-its-agent-starts-at
                      seon.procs-test/a-fork-at-another-commit-is-recorded-misaligned}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-proof-run-ends-with-its-agent"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by step E's paid run 4 (2026-10-22, worktree dry-run) and again 2026-10-23 (a proof fork at 80% CPU for 51 minutes under ppid 1, load 60): an agent's proof run (`seon.agent/run-tests-forked`, a fork of the agent) outlived its agent. The child learned of its agent's death only at its next write to the pipe (EPIPE), which a polling or sleeping test never makes. Built: the run's channel is a socketpair (`seon.sock/socketpair`) and the child watches its end exactly as an agent watches its socket to root (task seon.agent/an-agent-ends-with-its-root): `seon.sock/signal-at-input!` and `seon.intr/exit-at-eof!` (SIGIO at the next safe point, the child exits 0 only when `peer-gone?` peeks EOF). The agent's end closes when the agent dies, however it dies. No clock, no group, no second mechanism. Proof: seon.agent-test/a-proof-run-ends-with-its-agent (a proof run parked in a sleep holding a fifo's write end; its agent SIGKILLed; the fifo's reader sees EOF, not the byte the run writes if it outlives its agent). Red before (byte after the sleep), green after. Wiki: docs/failure-points.md (an agent dies while its proof run runs)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-proof-run-ends-with-its-agent}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-agent-ends-with-its-root"
  :seon.task/plan "An agent is ended when its run ends, and an agent whose root is gone has none (AGENTS §4). Reproduction: five `./seon root` processes with parent 1 and cwd /private/tmp/seon-agents (:seon.config.agent/cwd), up to 2h12m old, left by test roots; SIGTERM did nothing (they waited in pthread_cond_wait), SIGKILL ended them. What exists, with no clock: (1) the fork server (`seon.fork-server/main`) reads EOF on its socket to root and kills its process group (`lib/c-kill 0 9`), which holds every agent, clone, test child and standby; (2) an agent reads EOF on its own socket to root (`seon.agent/recv-raw!`, `next-msg!`) and exits (`seon.repl/serve!`, `await-op!`); (3) a busy agent: `seon.sock/signal-at-input!` raises SIGIO when its socket becomes readable, and `seon.intr/exit-at-eof!` ends the process when `seon.sock/peer-gone?` reads EOF (armed in `seon.agent/init!` and in a clone; its failure point `:seon.sock/unwatched` is sent with the hello and recorded as the agent's problem; docs/failure-points.md). A process outlives its root only when both (1) and (2) fail: its fork server killed by pid before root, and the agent not reading its socket. Cleanups therefore kill a root or its fork server's group (`kill -9 -PGID`), never a fork server by pid (AGENTS §8; bin/worktrees names each orphan's group). Open: an agent blocked in a wait (`(<!! (timeout 600000))`, `(<!! (chan))`) has its main thread in Chez's S_condition_wait, which reaches no safe point, so neither SIGIO nor root's SIGUSR1 runs until the wait returns; it is covered by the group kill until seon.intr/a-signal-wakes-a-blocked-wait merges. Not built: a kqueue NOTE_EXIT watcher on root's pid (a second mechanism beside the socket's EOF, needing a thread in a process that must stay single-threaded to fork). Proof: a root with agents (idle, one in a long wait, one in a long eval, a clone), SIGKILL of the root and its fork server and standby, then the fork server's process group is empty (`pgrep -g`). Wiki: docs/agent.md."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/an-agent-ends-when-its-root-is-gone seon.agent-test/a-blocked-agent-ends-with-its-root}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-eval-error-carries-its-data"
  :seon.task/plan "`seon.agent/error-map` keeps an eval error's ex-data as printed text (:data, cut at the part budget), which the error's render then prints again as a quoted string. Done: the ex-data travels as data, its diagnostic keys (Jolt's :clojure.error/* and :jolt.error/line, column, symbol, ns, kind, suggestions) as keys of the error map under the :seon.agent/eval-error schema, the suggestions as :seon.error/fix, other ex-data as :data (a map); the unresolved-symbol message is built from :jolt.error/symbol (no suffix to print twice); `seon.render.ui/error-shown` holds :jolt.error/suggestions so the render prints it once. Deletes the printed :data string and the double print. Wiki: errors#an-eval-errors-diagnostics."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/an-eval-errors-data-is-printed-once seon.repl-test/an-ordinary-exception-is-its-error-map seon.repl-test/an-eval-error-says-where-in-its-forms-own-text seon.agent-test/a-compile-errors-line-and-suggestions-arrive-as-data}}
 {:seon.task/name "messages-are-facts"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `seon.agent/messages` takes from the process's :inbox atom; root's :message request appends to it; `my.agent/send-message` goes through root. A message vanishes with the agent, nothing records it, and the owner has no input bar (parity.md 'my.message', '/agent/{id}/message'). v1: messages were facts with received-tx custody, and an unread message woke a turn (~/src/seon/src/my/message.clj). Correct: root writes :seon.message/{id,from,to,body} on the recipient's branch; `my.agent/messages` reads the unreceived ones and records their receipt; an unread message on an idle agent is an event the reconciler sees; the agent page's bar posts one through /call. Deletes: the :inbox atom path for messages (fx replies keep theirs)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.web/call-checks-the-origin"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-message-survives-the-agents-death seon.web-test/the-bar-posts-a-message-fact}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-spawn-says-where-its-time-went"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: root records `:spawned` (with the fork server's `:fork-ms`) and `:ready` (`seon.agent-proc`, `:start-ms` from the agent's `seon.agent/hello!`); nothing in between is visible. So a 30x spawn regression (the memory probe walking a 64 GiB map) showed only as a larger :start-ms and was found by `sample` by hand. Correct: each process stamps `seon.profile/now-ns` (one timeline across processes) at fixed points: root's request, the writer's branch (`seon.writer` :spawn), the fork server's fork, and the child's `seon.db/after-fork!`, connect, ns, memory probe and hello. The child sends its stamps as `:steps` in the hello it already sends, so no new message. Root's agent proc writes one `:spawn-timeline` event per agent, and `bin/check scale` prints p50/p95 per step. Deletes: nothing; replaces reading spawn cost from :start-ms alone."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/a-spawn-records-each-steps-time}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-boot-interrupt-check-is-read-or-goes"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: every agent boot runs `seon.agent/interrupt-self-test` (agent.clj:265-275): it signals itself SIGUSR1 and evals up to 100,000 times until its handler runs. The result travels in the hello (:interrupt-ok, agent.clj:611 -> repl.clj:406 -> seon.agent-proc :130) and nothing reads it. Correct: a false result is a recorded problem (its owner the agent proc, as a death is), or the check is deleted with its hello key. Deletes the unread key either way."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/an-agent-whose-interrupt-handler-never-ran-is-a-problem}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-agent-reports-its-threads"
  :seon.task/plan "Filed by worktree session agent-view (task seon.render/a-debug-view-of-any-agent, 2026-10-23): the owner asked the agent's page to show its process's threads and uptime. The agent's :stats answer (seon.agent/handle! :stats) and its hello carry pid, memory and t, no thread count; root's view entry has no start stamp (its run has :seon.run/start-ms, which the page shows). Correct: the agent reports its OS thread count with its memory (a fact measured, as mem-mb is), root's view keeps it, and the page's head shows it; the head shows the run's start instead of a ticking uptime (a clock deciding a render)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/an-agent-reports-its-threads}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-agent-edits-a-form-by-its-text"
  :seon.task/plan "WAITS FOR ROOT'S GATE (AGENTS §3: a new function for agents); do not build before it. What a plain REPL does: it has no edit; a change is the whole form typed again (`(defn f ...)` replaces the var, and Seon records that form as the branch's change, docs/source.md). For a long form that is the whole text sent again to change one line, and the retyped copy can drift from the original elsewhere. Correct: `(my.agent/edit! 'ns/var old new)` takes the var's recorded :form/src, replaces `old` with `new` where `old` occurs exactly once, and evaluates the result through the one eval path (the same recording, ticks and render as the typed form). It is not a guard (AGENTS §4: get out of an agent's way): it refuses nothing a plain REPL would run; an `old` that occurs zero or several times is an answered error as data (the count and the form-relative positions of each occurrence, `:seon.error/at` coordinates), because the request cannot be carried out, as `(nth v 9)` answers an index error; the agent can always type the whole form. The var is a name the one resolver reads (`:var/name`), the match is exact text, never a line number (they shift after an edit). Replaces: resending a whole form to change part of it; measured as bytes sent per change on a 60-line function. Cost proof: work done is the form's bytes, one replace and one eval, linear in the form. Proofs: an edit changes one occurrence and the var evaluates; a missing or repeated `old` answers the typed error with its positions; the edit is recorded as the form's change. Needs the error-location task merged (`:seon.error/at`)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.agent/an-eval-error-carries-its-data"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/an-edit-replaces-one-occurrence-in-a-form seon.agent-test/an-edit-that-matches-nothing-or-twice-says-where}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-resumed-branch-holds-what-main-gained"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by step E run #11 (2026-10-23, run seon.context/e11-map-from-program#2 on next-writeback-order's root): a resumed kept branch runs ```seon.program/load-diff!``` from main as forked (db0) to the branch head (```seon.agent/init!```, agent.clj ~714), so every definition main gained after the branch point is unmapped: main had imported test/ since the branch was made, and the agent held seon.context-test with no vars, so the design never reached the proof's fixtures and spent 27 turns reading seon.db. Any resumed branch behind main runs without what main gained, silently. Correct: a resumed branch behind main (:seon.merge/behind) merges main first, through the one merge path, then load-diff! loads only the branch's own changes; loading from the merge base alone would leave the branch db without main's forms (test-plan, reach digests). Proof: make a branch, import a namespace into main, resume the branch: the agent holds that namespace's vars as main does."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-resumed-branch-holds-what-main-gained}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-proof-run-that-never-comes-is-a-recorded-fact"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-23 (shrink): `seon.agent-test/a-proof-run-ends-with-its-agent` hung `bin/test seon.agent-test` for 17-54 minutes in four checkouts. Cause: the test called `seon.agent/run-tests-forked` with two arguments after the ruling-(a) commit d6d93b47 made it `[ticks proofs mode ...]`; the agent answered an `invalid-arity` contract error (right, as data), and the test, whose only evidence is a byte on a fifo, blocked in `open(2)` of the fifo for reading, which waits for a writer that never comes. The call is fixed (the mode `:turn`, as at the call in a-proof-run-ends-at-its-budget...). Owed: a test that can hang is a defect. The run that never comes must end the test as a recorded fact with no timeout: the form's reply is on the event tap (`:reply` with `:items`), and the proof run's start is a fact only if the call goes through the `forked` hook (`run-tests-forked`'s 4th argument sends `:tests-started` with the child's pid, as `seon.agent/run-tests-forked` does for the test service); the test awaits that event or an error reply on its tap, then opens the fifo. Same class: other string-built calls to contracted functions in test/ fail only when run; `bin/check` can resolve the calls' vars and arities from the compiler's facts instead. Proofs: calling with the old two arguments fails the test at once, naming the agent's arity reply (red), and passes with the mode (green)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-proof-run-ends-with-its-agent}}
 {:seon.task/name "one-test-bound-derived-and-named"
  :seon.task/plan "Done: a test run is bounded by its work, never a clock: `seon.agent/run-tests-forked` runs the proofs within `:seon.config.agent/test-ticks` engine ticks (named in docs/failure-points.md; bin/check robust asserts that a test that never ends is stopped with :seon.test/ticks-spent naming it). The child writes {:seon.test/started var} as each test var begins (clojure.test's :begin-test-var report, which Jolt emits) and its result as the last line; `seon.agent/test-run-answer` turns the last line and the exit status into the run: the result, or one :error report on the started var with :exit, so `seon.proofs/results` reads that test :error and the context shows a red proof with the death beside it. Deleted: the 30000 ms literals at 8 sites, the :timeout-ms forwarding in `seon.root/ext-op`, the deadline loop of run-tests-forked, and `seon.root/fs-call!`'s 30000 ms deref fallback. The ticks are not a clock and stay: without them robust's endless test hangs. A run that hangs otherwise ends only by an explicit kill of the agent (a stall is task seon.root/a-stall-is-past-the-requests-own-bound). v1 bounded each test by a declared per-test bound (default 5 s, seon.test/bounded-result, ~/src/seon)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-killed-test-run-names-the-test-that-was-running
                      seon.web-test/calls-become-requests}}
 {:seon.task/name "fork-error-names-the-threads"
  :seon.task/plan "Done in part: `seon.lib/fork!` forks only while this is the one active Chez thread and otherwise calls its :refused fn at once with the active thread count, so a test run beside a thread answers :seon.test/fork-refused {:active-threads n} (`seon.agent/run-tests-forked`) instead of the old safe-fork's 1 s spin. Left: `seon.agent`'s interrupt-self-test spins up to 100,000 evals waiting for its own SIGUSR1. Correct: the self-test waits on one safe point (one engine tick) and records whether the handler ran. Deletes the 100,000. Its two proofs are not yet written."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-fork-beside-a-thread-is-refused-at-once-naming-it
                      seon.agent-test/the-interrupt-self-test-needs-one-safe-point}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-test-green-under-the-runner-is-green-in-an-agent"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by a worked example (evaluations session, 2026-10-23): seon.repl-test/an-error-in-an-anonymous-fn-is-in-the-fn-around-it and seon.repl-test/an-eval-error-says-where-in-its-forms-own-text are green under bin/test on main (cb77a911), but red when run with clojure.test/test-vars at any agent's REPL, on main or on a branch (6 assertions: :seon.error/at and :seon.error/in differ, e.g. {:line 1} for {:line 3}, rewrite-clj.zip.seqz/map-seq for nil); the judge's fork of the agent ran the first red too, and the done rule held the example's run for it as a stray red (with seon.schema-test/register-validates-against-the-population). A test whose verdict depends on where it runs judges nothing: an agent's branch is held for a red its change did not cause. Find the difference (the agent loads its program from the branch's recorded forms, the runner from files: an error's position and the frames Jolt reports may differ), fix it at its producer, so a test's verdict in an agent and its fork equals the runner's."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-outside-runs-scratch-stays-scratch"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by a worked example (evaluations session, 2026-10-23; notes example-1 in agent-view's notes folder): to call a test namespace's private helper an agent ran (in-ns 'seon.repl-test) and defined scratch values there (seon.repl-test/changed, eval-code-src, long-bad); the branch recorded them as definitions of that namespace, so its merge would carry them (they showed under :will-merge :added). Nothing an agent can call takes a definition back off its branch: ns-unmap and ```seon.program/remove-definition!``` unmap only in the process. Correct: an agent can undo a definition it made on its branch (recorded as its removal, which the merge carries as one), and the context tells it to work in its own namespace and reach a private helper as @#'ns/f. Proof: an agent that defines a value in a program namespace and removes it leaves nothing of it in what its merge carries."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "await-reply-keeps-other-replies"
  :seon.task/plan "From the branch review of jolt/agent-conversation (2026-10-25, root's scratchpad branch-review.md, follow-ups): `seon.agent/await-reply!` pushes replies for other keys into :pending, which are later handled as unknown ops. Correct: a reply for another awaited key is kept for that awaiter and answered to it; nothing is read as an op it is not. Proof: two awaits in flight, their replies out of order, each answered to its own."
  :seon.task/kind :work}]
